# id: M79-read-index-answers-at-a-clock-reading
# covering-test: TestRaftExitCriteria
# package: ./sim/hunt/
# expect: killed
# power-seeds: 200
# power-floor: 17
# power-ceiling: 20
# power-measured: 35 of 200, first at seed 0, under CURRENT (A7's shape); and 74 of 600, first at seed 0, under a7-nosplit. The FIRST version of this patch read the clock at ANSWER time instead of arrival and measured 0 of 400 under current and 0 of 600 under a7-nosplit -- a 74x difference between two mutants that both looked like they planted the same defect. The shape was not what hid it; the timeline was
# mutates: a plain read served by read index is answered at a TIMESTAMP taken from the serving replica's own clock WHEN THE REQUEST ARRIVED, rather than at the latest version. This is BUG-028, the second member of the read-is-a-log-entry class, and the one that makes the class worth enumerating: the argument that let a plain read leave the log is D-A7-5's sentence 'a PLAIN read has no timestamp to protect', and the implementation gave it one anyway. ReadAt returns the newest version AT OR BEFORE ts, so under skew inside maxOffset a follower's clock sits below the leader's and a write the leader stamped above that follower's clock is invisible -- however far past the confirmed index the follower has applied. Read index's whole claim is that it is correct WITHOUT trusting clocks; answering at a clock reading puts the clock back in. THE ARRIVAL TIME IS LOAD-BEARING: a first attempt planted the clock read at ANSWER time instead, one line and three hunks smaller, and measured 0 of 400 -- because by the time a read is answered the serving replica has absorbed the leader's clock through the messages that carried the confirmed index. A mutant that plants 'the same defect' one step later in the timeline plants a different and weaker defect. Killed by read-index-answers-match-the-log, whose model reads the LATEST version for an off-log answer for exactly this reason; per-key linearizability catches it only when some client observed the write that was missed
